import static bootcamp.Check.*;

import java.util.*;

class Exercise {

    enum Status {
        DRAFT, PLACED, PAID, SHIPPED, CANCELLED;

        /** Whether this status may change to target. */
        boolean canMoveTo(Status target) {
            return switch (this) {
                case DRAFT -> target == PLACED || target == CANCELLED;
                case PLACED -> target == PAID || target == CANCELLED;
                case PAID -> target == SHIPPED || target == CANCELLED;
                case SHIPPED, CANCELLED -> false;
            };
        }

        /** target when allowed, otherwise IllegalStateException. */
        Status moveTo(Status target) {
            if (!canMoveTo(target)) throw new IllegalStateException(this + " cannot move to " + target);
            return target;
        }

        /** A status name in any case, surrounding whitespace allowed. */
        static Status parse(String code) {
            if (code == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("status is required");
            return Status.valueOf(code.strip().toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT));
        }
    }

    // ─── tests ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        test("the happy path is allowed", () -> ok(Status.DRAFT.canMoveTo(Status.PLACED) && Status.PLACED.canMoveTo(Status.PAID) && Status.PAID.canMoveTo(Status.SHIPPED), "draft, placed, paid, shipped"));
        test("cancel is allowed only before shipping", () -> ok(Status.PAID.canMoveTo(Status.CANCELLED) && !Status.SHIPPED.canMoveTo(Status.CANCELLED), "cancel rules"));
        test("nothing leaves a final status, and no self-moves (boundary)", () -> ok(!Status.SHIPPED.canMoveTo(Status.DRAFT) && !Status.CANCELLED.canMoveTo(Status.PLACED) && !Status.PAID.canMoveTo(Status.PAID), "final states"));
        test("moveTo returns the target or throws", () -> { eq(Status.PLACED.moveTo(Status.PAID), Status.PAID); raises(IllegalStateException.class, () -> Status.SHIPPED.moveTo(Status.DRAFT)); });
        test("parse ignores case and whitespace", () -> eq(Status.parse("  paid "), Status.PAID));
        test("parse rejects unknown and null codes", () -> { raises(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -> Status.parse("refunded")); raises(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -> Status.parse(null)); });
        System.exit(report());
    }
    // ─── end tests ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
}

drills.explanation:
Let the current constant decide its legal targets in one switch over `this`, and make the other methods consult it:

```java
boolean canMoveTo(Status target) {
    return switch (this) {
        case DRAFT -> target == PLACED || target == CANCELLED;
        case PLACED -> target == PAID || target == CANCELLED;
        case PAID -> target == SHIPPED || target == CANCELLED;
        case SHIPPED, CANCELLED -> false;
    };
}
```

The arms name every constant, so a new status does not compile until someone decides its moves. A shortcut such as "the next ordinal, or CANCELLED" instead of explicit arms fails "cancel is allowed only before shipping": `CANCELLED` is declared right after `SHIPPED`, so a shipped order could be cancelled. `moveTo` must throw `IllegalStateException`, not `IllegalArgumentException`, because the current state forbids the move; "moveTo returns the target or throws" checks the type. `parse` strips and upper-cases with `Locale.ROOT` before `valueOf`. Without the explicit null check, `code.strip()` throws NullPointerException and "parse rejects unknown and null codes" fails, since it expects IllegalArgumentException. Unknown names need no extra code: `valueOf` already throws IllegalArgumentException for them.

